    "case_id": "644",
    "case_summary": "A 39-year-old man with a 2-year history of episodic hypertension and micturition-related syncope. He reported several years of episodic chest pain, palpitations, and diaphoresis. His symptoms included persistent micturition-related episodes of headaches, diaphoresis, palpitations, chest pain, and presyncope.\n\nOn arrival, vital signs showed:- Tachycardia (112 beats/min) - Blood pressure 196/120 mmHg- Well oxygenated on mechanical ventilation at 70% FiO2- Pink, frothy secretions from endotracheal tube- Bilateral crackles on pulmonary examination- Significant blood pressure lability noted overnight\n\nLaboratory Tests:- High-sensitivity troponin T: 936 ng/L, peaked at 1026 ng/L- N-terminal pro-BNP: 49 pg/mL- Plasma free metanephrine levels: isolated elevation in plasma normetanephrine (36 nmol/L)- 24-hour urinary metanephrines: isolated normetanephrine elevation (8990 mcg)- Free norepinephrine: 21,685 pg/mL, with normal epinephrine and dopamine levelsImaging Studies:- ECG: sinus tachycardia without ST-T wave changes- Chest radiography: bilateral hilar opacities suggesting acute pulmonary edema- Transthoracic echocardiogram: mildly depressed left ventricular systolic function (EF 40-45%) with hypokinesis of septum and inferolateral wall- Cardiac catheterization: normal filling pressures, no coronary artery disease- Renal artery Doppler ultrasound: unremarkable- CT abdomen: normal adrenal glands, 1.9 cm × 2.8 cm contrast-enhancing mass in bladder wall with surrounding lymphadenopathy",
